${ENV}

Read docs/ARCHITECTURE.md (especially section 5 "Composition contract"), common/pipeline_core/*.py, and then the actual source of each package: hybrid_retrieval_model/hybrid_retrieval, task_decomposition_model/task_decomposition, answer_verification_model/answer_verification, parallelization_model/parallelization, caching_model/caching, pipeline_rag_model/pipeline_rag (read model.py, __init__.py, config.py and the README of each). Their public APIs as reported by their authors:

${apiSummary}

Deliverables (write only inside examples/):
1. examples/enterprise_pipeline.py - an EnterprisePipeline class plus a runnable main(). Wiring: one shared Tracer; CachingModel wraps the LLM client (CachedLLMClient) so every component shares the cache; HybridRetrievalModel indexes request documents and acts as the Retriever; ParallelizationModel provides the Executor; AnswerVerificationModel acts as the Verifier; PipelineRAGModel receives retriever, verifier, executor; TaskDecompositionModel decomposes complex tasks and uses rag.run (or an adapter) as its SubTaskSolver, executing independent sub-tasks through the executor; a final verification pass gates the synthesised answer; results carry rolled-up usage and the full span tree (tracer.tree()). Include a complexity router: simple questions go straight to RAG, complex ones through decomposition. Provide a small built-in engineering corpus (e.g. 8-12 short documents about a pump station design: specs, test logs, standards clauses, incident report) and two demo tasks (one simple factual, one complex multi-part design review) run with domain="mechanical_engineering". Must run offline: python examples/enterprise_pipeline.py --offline (use make_llm(); it returns MockLLM without credentials) and print a readable report including the span tree and usage.
2. examples/tests/test_enterprise_pipeline.py - offline tests proving the wiring: components receive each other (isinstance against pipeline_core protocols), cache is shared (second identical run has cache_hits > 0 or cached=True), usage rolls up, span tree includes every component name, both demo tasks complete with status in (SUCCESS, PARTIAL, NEEDS_REVIEW, CACHED) and non-empty output_text.
3. Run: cd "${REPO}" && python -m pytest examples/tests -q and PYTHONPATH="common:hybrid_retrieval_model:task_decomposition_model:answer_verification_model:parallelization_model:caching_model:pipeline_rag_model" python examples/enterprise_pipeline.py --offline . Both must succeed. Set sys.path inside the example so it also runs without PYTHONPATH when executed from the repo root.

If a package's API makes wiring awkward or impossible, work around it locally in the example and record the friction precisely in api_issues (file, what is wrong, suggested change) - do not edit the packages. In pipeline_description, describe the exact data flow for the main README author.`, { label: 'integrate:enterprise_pipeline', phase: 'Integrate', schema: INTEGRATION_SCHEMA, effort: 'high' })

return {
  packages: done.map(r => ({
    pkg: r.model.pkg,
